  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Warming Trend into the Week.\n\nA mid-level ridge will progress slowly eastward this week, with\nits center possibly over the southern Mid-Atlantic by Thursday.\nThe increased heights will support a continual warming trend \nwith high temperatures potentially 10 to 20 degrees above normal\nthan seasonal averages. Temperatures will be in the 80s and 90s\nwith parts of the Piedmont reaching 100 degrees by Thursday \nafternoon. This, combined with dew points in the 60s to lower \n70s, will result in some high heat indices. The maximum heat \nindex east of the Blue Ridge could get into the lower 100s. If \nconfidence increases of these values reaching 105 degrees or \nhigher, Heat Advisories will be needed. Even if this criteria is\nnot reached, these are very hot temperatures for September \nstandards and the public is encouraged to stay cool and hydrated\nto avoid any heat related illnesses. A cold front may arrive by\nthis weekend to lower some of the temperatures.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Next best chance of precipitation by Friday.\n\nThe mid-level ridge over the Mid-Atlantic will degrade and \npossibly split in two by this weekend. The synoptic set up \nacross CONUS will then be a strong ridge over the Great Plains \nto our west and a potent mid-level low to the northeast in New \nEngland and Canada. This will bring northwesterly flow to the \nMid-Atlantic and permit the passage of any shortwaves traveling\nin between the ridge and trough. One of these disturbances may \npass through by Friday and supply a widespread chance of \nprecipitation. The instability will likely be present with warm \nand humid conditions persisting, but the better wind shear looks\nto be to our north. Lapse rates as well look to be \nunderwhelming and ranging between 5.5-6.0 C/km. Some machine \nlearning models still highlight this area for severe weather \nlater in the week but much of the convection and its \norganization will be based on how the upper air pattern \nmanifests. If the northwesterly flow pushes a little southward \nand allow for better wind shear and cooler temperatures aloft, \nthen confidence would increase for severe weather potential. \nUntil then, this will be a weather setup to observe in the \ncoming days.",
